From 9a902403a2929b5fb396cbee07cb6d918290648b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ferdinand Vanmaele <info@fvanmaele.de>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 09:50:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 14/19] [clangd] Index navigation: answer once the shards are
 loaded, off the worker pool

The index path of go-to-definition and find-references (navigation from
the index) asked the index every 50 ms for up to 5 s and answered as soon
as it knew the symbol. At startup the background index adds its stored
shards one project batch at a time, so the first references found were
taken as the answer: a first find-references after a restart could list
3 of 40 references, and the complete AST answer then lost the race.

The index path now waits until every project the compile database has
handed over is loaded into the index (BackgroundIndex::waitForLoadedShards,
counted from enqueue() to the end of loadProject(), after the rebuild) and
then asks once. It waits on its own thread (AsyncTaskRunner) with the
request's context, not on a TUScheduler worker: the poller held a slot of
the semaphore the AST build it races needs, and ignored cancellation. The
wait ends when the request is answered or cancelled, or the server stops
(the destructor waits for these threads first). The shard is now read on
that thread, not on the main thread. An index answer that locates nothing
leaves the answer to the AST. The wait is a named constant; the log says
why the index did not answer.
